Peer scripts transcribed from real servers on the public internet.

Each file is a dialogue-peer(1) script replaying, verbatim, what a public
server actually answered when the shipped protocols.cfg entry of the same
name spoke to it. Recorded 2026-08-31; the host and port are in each
header. They exist so the shipped entries are checked against the wording
real servers use -- multi-line 250s, tagged IMAP replies, the exact
closing codes -- and not only against what a test author imagined.

The client's own IP, which several servers echo back, is replaced by
203.0.113.1 (RFC 5737 TEST-NET-3). Session identifiers are left as
recorded; they are random per connection and mean nothing here.

Re-record with record.py in this directory; it speaks each entry and writes
the transcript beside it.

record.py speaks the line-oriented protocols and decodes what comes back as
text, which is right for all of these but telnet: that one negotiates in
IAC byte sequences, so its transcript was captured raw and is rendered with
the negotiation decoded and every other non-printable byte as \xNN.
